Benson's win odds around 90-95 percent", "Open seat in an even-lean state; James is a strong two-time statewide recruit, but every major rater calls the race Lean D or better"], "rationale": "Benson leads the polling average by about seven to nine points across a dozen recent surveys, has doubled James's fundraising, and markets price her odds above ninety percent, while raters uniformly place the race at Lean D or better. Republican-sponsored polls and James's statewide profile suggest a somewhat tighter race, but the weight of evidence supports a clear but not safe Democratic edge.", "watch": "If economic discontent consolidates behind James in the final weeks, as the tighter partisan-sponsored polls hint, the margin could compress toward mid-single digits and put the outcome genuinely in play.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gretchen Whitmer (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's governorship is open as Gretchen Whitmer terms out, and Democrat Jocelyn Benson faces Republican John James in a contest that raters uniformly call at least Lean D, with Silver Bulletin alone rating it Likely D. Recent polling has been plentiful and consistent: a dozen surveys since late August put Benson ahead by margins ranging from one to seventeen points, with most high-quality pollsters showing leads of five to twelve points and published averages clustering near seven to nine.\n\nBenson has raised roughly twice James's money and holds a large cash advantage heading into the final stretch, and headlines flag a Republican ad-spending deficit. Prediction markets across three platforms price her chances around ninety percent or better. James, a two-time statewide contender, keeps the race competitive on paper in a state with an even partisan lean, but the combined weight of polling, money, and expert ratings points to a Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9339, "margin": 7.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9166, "margin": 7